{"command": "cd /testbed && cat > /tmp/research.md <<'EOF'\n# Phase 0 Research: Batch Journal Writer for TinyDB\n\nFeature: `specs/001-transactions/spec.md` | Date: 2026-10-17\n\n## Unknowns from Technical Context and Their Resolution\n\nNo `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` markers remained in the spec; the unknowns below are\nthe technical decisions the plan had to make, resolved from direct inspection of\nthe codebase (evidence: file/line references).\n\n### D1 - How to make a batch atomic without changing TinyDB's ordinary APIs\n\n- **Decision**: `BatchWriter.apply` performs one read-modify-write per batch\n  against the default table's storage, instead of calling `table.insert` /\n  `table.remove` per operation.\n- **Rationale**: `Table._update_table` (tinydb/table.py:804) already implements the\n  only write path: `storage.read()` -> mutate the table dict -> `storage.write()`.\n  `MemoryStorage.read()` (tinydb/storages.py:177) returns the live in-memory dict\n  with **no copy**, so per-op writes would mutate shared state mid-batch and a\n  later failure would leave partial changes. A single in-memory pass over a\n  deep-copied snapshot, committed with one `storage.write`, gives true atomicity\n  with no changes to `Table`/`TinyDB`/`Storage` public behavior (Constitution I).\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  1. Call `table.insert`/`table.remove` per op and restore on error. Rejected:\n     `Table.remove(doc_ids=...)` (tinydb/table.py:608) *silently skips* missing\n     IDs, so a missing-ID remove would not raise as R02 requires; also each op\n     does a full storage round-trip and mutates state incrementally.\n  2. Wrap each op in try/except and call `storage.write(snapshot)` on failure.\n     Rejected: still leaves the `_next_id` cache and per-op overhead; the single\n     pass is simpler and strictly more atomic.\n\n### D2 - ID allocation must match TinyDB's own rule\n\n- **Decision**: Replicate `Table._get_next_id` (tinydb/table.py:751): if the\n  table is empty the first ID is 1; otherwise it is `max(existing ids) + 1`,\n  computed against the in-batch working dict so later ops see earlier inserts.\n- **Rationale**: Keeps returned IDs identical to what ordinary sequential\n  `table.insert` calls would produce (acceptance test expects `[1]` for the first\n  insert). No reliance on the private `Table._next_id` cache.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Reusing `Table._get_next_id` directly - rejected\n  because it reads storage and mutates the private `_next_id` cache, which would\n  desynchronize from a rolled-back snapshot.\n\n### D3 - Rollback must be observable by previously acquired table handles\n\n- **Decision**: On error, `storage.write(deepcopy(pre_batch_snapshot))`. On\n  success, `storage.write(working_tables)`. Both go through the same storage\n  write path ordinary writes use.\n- **Rationale**: `TinyDB.table()` caches `Table` instances (tinydb/database.py:111)\n  and every read goes through `Table._read_table` -> `storage.read()`\n  (tinydb/table.py:771), so any handle re-reads storage on each access. A write\n  to storage is therefore visible to all handles without re-acquisition (R02/FR-008).\n  The query cache is per-table and only used by `search`; `all()`/`__iter__`/\n  `__len__` always re-read storage (tinydb/table.py:221,715), so no stale-handle\n  risk for the operations the requirements exercise.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Manually invalidating each known table's query\n  cache - unnecessary for the exercised read paths and would couple the writer to\n  private cache internals.\n\n### D4 - No aliasing between caller input and stored/returned data\n\n- **Decision**: Deep-copy each inserted document **before** it enters the working\n  table (`copy.deepcopy(document)`), and return IDs only (no document objects).\n- **Rationale**: `Table.insert` stores `dict(document)` (tinydb/table.py:168), a\n  *shallow* copy - nested structures would still alias the caller (violates R03).\n  Deep-copying in the writer guarantees `mutating the caller's dict after apply`\n  cannot touch stored data. Because only IDs are returned, there is no returned\n  document object to alias. (Constitution IV: minimal change - deep copy is the\n  smallest mechanism that satisfies R03.)\n- **Alternatives considered**: Relying on `Table.insert`'s shallow copy - rejected\n  (insufficient for nested data).\n\n### D5 - Validation rules and error type\n\n- **Decision**: Validate each operation **before** any mutation, raising\n  `ValueError` for: non-dict operation; `op` not in `{'insert','remove'}`;\n  insert missing `document` or `document` not a `Mapping`; remove missing\n  `doc_id`, or `doc_id` not an `int` (bool excluded) or `<= 0`. A remove whose\n  `doc_id` is absent from the working table at its turn is also `ValueError`.\n- **Rationale**: Matches R01/R02 and spec FR-005/FR-007; bool is a subclass of\n  int in Python so it is explicitly excluded (assumption A2).\n- **Alternatives considered**: Raising `KeyError`/`LookupError` for missing IDs -\n  rejected; the issue and spec fix the error type as `ValueError`.\n\n### D6 - Where the code lives and how it is tested\n\n- **Decision**: New module `tinydb/journal.py` exposing `BatchWriter`.\n  New test file `tests/test_journal.py` (own tests). `acceptance_public.py`\n  and all existing `tests/*.py` remain untouched.\n- **Rationale**: The issue names `tinydb/journal.py` as the implementation\n  source; adding a test file does not modify upstream tests (Constitution I/II).\n- **Alternatives considered**: Extending `tinydb/table.py` - rejected; the issue\n  specifies a dedicated module and keeps the change additive.\n\n## Best-Practice Notes\n\n- Atomicity via snapshot + single commit is the standard in-memory transaction\n  pattern for read-modify-write stores (TinyDB's storage API only supports\n  whole-database writes, per the docstring of `Table._update_table`).\n- Keep the public surface minimal: one class, one method (`apply`), one return\n  type (`l